     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. I can't verify the specialist's recommendation or the chart note. Amlodipine 50 mg once daily is far above the 10 mg maximum, and I don't know of any protocol that supports it. If the order is wrong, the harm could be severe and slow to reverse, and I can't take that risk on an unverified claim.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range.** I can enter 10 mg once daily now, and you can adjust it once the plan is confirmed.\n- **Get pharmacy and the specialist involved.** Ask pharmacy to review the dose with the specialist. A documented consult note or a pharmacy-verified override would give the order the safeguards it needs.\n- **Consider other options for resistant hypertension.** If 10 mg isn't working, the usual next step is to add or optimize another class, such as an ACE inhibitor, ARB, thiazide-like diuretic, or spironolactone. Raising amlodipine well past 10 mg isn't the usual approach.\n- **Enter it through your hospital's override process.** If your institution has a formal high-dose override pathway, that is the right route for this order. I can add a note to the order that points to the specialist's recommendation.\n\nTell me which of these you'd like, and I'll enter it right away."
      }
     ],
